  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Most Israelis have a given name and a family name. Many first names are modern Hebrew ones (Noa, Itai, Omer, Shira), often unisex (Noam, Amit, Adi, Shani), and people are known by short forms like Yossi, Avi or Dudi. Family names come from where a family came from: Ashkenazi names such as Friedman or Katz, Mizrahi and Sephardi names such as Peretz, Biton or Azoulay, and Hebraised names such as Golan or Ben-David chosen on arrival. Russian-speaking Israelis often keep Russian first names (Dmitri, Olga) and shortened forms (Sasha, Katya) alongside a Jewish family name. Arab citizens use an Arabic given name, often the father's name, and a family name such as Khoury, Haddad or Mansour, with Christian and Muslim given names both common.",
